From 5d0cf4783e17871ddd2227f907b543f0fe9906c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 13:05:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] assurance.report: the page the buyer reads, and a worked
 fleet to fill it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WHY
  Seven patches of engines, 504 tests, and every single output is terminal text
  or JSON. The only person who can see any of it is an engineer at a prompt.
  The people who decide whether this gets bought — a plant manager, a quality
  director, an insurer, a notified body, procurement — do not open terminals.
  They read documents, and they forward them. Until there is a document, this
  is a shell to everyone who matters commercially.

  And an engineer evaluating it has nowhere to start: seven domains, no worked
  example, twenty minutes of patience.

WHAT
  src/assurance/report/render.py  one self-contained HTML page: no network, no
                                   scripts, ~20 KB, opens in a plant with no
                                   internet, prints, attaches to a tender
  src/assurance/report/demo.py    a complete four-machine fleet built from
                                   nothing — real export files, real
                                   normalisation, real verification, real
                                   intervention records, chain verifying
  src/assurance/report/cli.py     demo / build
  src/assurance/api/fleet_routes  GET /v1/fleet/report (Cell plan), text/html

  Each demo machine is a different situation, because a demo where everything
  is fine demonstrates nothing: one clean and about to be hit by an advisory,
  one running an artefact that contradicts its own version label, one whose
  zone a technician reshaped with nothing re-run, one already remedied.

CUSTOMER VALUE
  `assurance report demo --out demo/` is the thirty-second answer to "what does
  this actually do", and report.html is the artefact an integrator forwards to
  their plant manager, their insurer and their notified body. That is the first
  thing in this whole package that a non-engineer can act on.

RISK
  The report is the surface most likely to be over-trusted, so three things are
  enforced by layout, not by good intentions. The ledger chain status is the
  first thing on the page — saying it at the bottom would be a lie of
  placement, and a broken chain is shouted in red above every finding. Findings
  are ordered by what somebody must do today, not by machine name. And "What
  this report does not establish" is a numbered section carrying every
  checks_skipped line from every engine verbatim. Data reaching the page is
  escaped; a machine name is attacker-controlled in a hosted deployment.

  The demo ledger carries a notice, in the evidence itself, naming it as
  demonstration data, so it can never be mistaken for a real one.

ROLLBACK
  git revert this commit. Outside assurance/report it adds one GET route, one
  CLI domain and one ruff ignore.

TESTS
  530 passed (26 new), ruff clean over src/assurance and the new tests.
  Asserted: the page reaches no network, the only URL in it is the advisory's
  own reference, the chain banner precedes every finding, a tampered ledger
  shouts before the fleet table, every engine limitation survives into the
  page, and injected HTML in a machine's site name is escaped.

SUCCESS CONDITION
  One integrator forwards report.html to somebody who would never have run a
  command, and that person asks a question about a specific serial number.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
